       [--lluummiinnoossiittyy | --ccoolloorrvvaalluuee | --ssaattuurraattiioonn]

       [_p_p_m_f_i_l_e]

       All  options  can  be abbreviated to their shortest unique prefix.  You
       may use two hyphens instead of one to designate an option.  You may use
       either  white  space  or  an equals sign between an option name and its
       value.


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmnnoorrmm reads a PNM image (PBM, PGM, or PPM).  It normalizes  the  con‐
       trast  by  forcing the brightest pixels to white, the darkest pixels to
       black, and spreading out the ones in between.   It  produces  the  same
       kind of file as output.  This is pretty useless for a PBM image.

       The  program offers two ways of spreading out the pixels in between the
       darkest and brightest: linear and quadratic.  In  the  quadratic  case,
       you specify some in between brightness and specify what brightness that
       should become in the output.  With those three constraints: the bright‐
       ness  that  becomes  black,  the brightness that becomes white, and the
       brightness that becomes that middle value, ppnnmmnnoorrmm computes a quadratic
       equation that maps all the other brightnesses from input values to out‐
       put values.

       The program first determines a mapping of old brightness to new bright‐
       ness.   For each possible brightness of a pixel, the program determines
       a corresponding brightness for the output image.

       Then for each pixel in the image, the program computes  a  color  which
       has  the desired output brightness and puts that in the output.  With a
       color image, it is not always possible to compute such a color and  re‐
       tain  any  semblance  of the original hue, so the brightest and dimmest
       pixels may only approximate the desired brightness.

       For a PPM image, you have a choice of three ways to define brightness:


       •      luminosity

       •      color value

       •      saturation


              In the case of saturation, "brightness" is pretty  much  a  mis‐
              nomer,  but  you  can  use the brightness analogy to see what it
              does.  In the analogy, bright means saturated and dark means un‐
              saturated.

       Note  that  all  of these are different from separately normalizing the
       individual color components.

       An alternative way to spread out the brightnesses in an  image  is  ppnn‐‐
       mmhhiisstteeqq.   ppnnmmhhiisstteeqq  stretches  the  brightest pixels to white and the
       darkest pixels to black, but rather than linearly adjusting the ones in
       between, it adjusts them so that there are an equal number of pixels of
       each brightness throughout the range.  This  gives  you  more  contrast
       than ppnnmmnnoorrmm does, but can considerably change the picture in exchange.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ),  ppnnmmnnoorrmm  recognizes  the
       following command line options:

       By  default,  the  darkest 2 percent of all pixels are mapped to black,
       and the brightest 1 percent are mapped to white.  You can override this
       behavior and specify either a different percentage, or specific bright‐
       ness values to map to black and to  white,  or  just  have  the  single
       greatest brightness map to white and the least brightness map to black.



       --bbppeerrcceenntt

       --wwppeerrcceenntt

       --bbvvaalluuee

       --wwvvaalluuee

              To  specify  a  percentage,  use the --bbppeerrcceenntt and --wwppeerrcceenntt op‐
              tions, or you can specify the exact pixel values to be mapped by
              using  the --bbvvaalluuee and --wwvvaalluuee options.  You can get appropriate
              numbers for the options from ppppmmhhiisstt.  If you just want  to  en‐
              hance  the  contrast,  then  choose values at elbows in the his‐
              togram; e.g. if value 29 represents 3% of the image but value 30
              represents  20%,  choose 30 for _b_v_a_l_u_e.  If you want to brighten
              the image, then set _b_v_a_l_u_e to 0 and  just  fiddle  with  _w_v_a_l_u_e;
              similarly,  to  darken  the image, set _w_v_a_l_u_e to maxval and play
              with _b_v_a_l_u_e.

              If you specify both --bbvvaalluuee and --bbppeerrcceenntt, ppnnmmnnoorrmm uses the  one
              that  produces  the least change.  The same goes for --wwvvaalluuee and
              --wwppeerrcceenntt.  (In Netpbm 10.26 (January 2005), the --bbvvaalluuee/--wwvvaalluuee
              takes  precedence, and before that, it's a syntax error to spec‐
              ify both).

              If you want to maximize the change  instead  of  minimizing  it,
              just  cascade  two  runs  of  ppnnmmnnoorrmm, specifying values for the
              first and percentages for the second.

              --bbppeerrcceenntt and --wwppeerrcceenntt values are floating point decimal.  Zero
              is valid and is the same as --bbvvaalluuee==00 or --wwvvaalluuee==_m_a_x_v_a_l, respec‐
              tively.

              Because there are whole numbers of pixels  at  each  brightness,
              ppnnmmnnoorrmm  obviously  can't  guarantee the exact percentage, so it
              arranges that _a_t _l_e_a_s_t the percentage of pixels you specify  get
              remapped as promised.

              It  is  possible for your --bbppeerrcceenntt or --wwppeerrcceenntt to overlap your
              --wwvvaalluuee or --bbvvaalluuee, respectively.  For example, you  say  --bbppeerr‐‐
              cceenntt==2200 and --wwvvaalluuee==110000 for an image in which only 10 percent of
              the pixels are darker than 100.  In that case,  ppnnmmnnoorrmm  adjusts
              the percentile value as required.  In the example, it uses 99 as
              the black value (like --bbvvaalluuee==9999).

              It is also possible for your --bbppeerrcceenntt and --wwppeerrcceenntt options  to
              select  the  same  brightness value for the stretch-to-white and
              stretch-to-black value because of the fact  that  ppnnmmnnoorrmm  can't
              subdivide  a histogram cell.  E.g. if an image is all brightness
              100, then no matter what  --bbppeerrcceenntt  and  --wwppeerrcceenntt  values  you
              choose,  it's  the  same  as saying --bbvvaalluuee==110000 --wwvvaalluuee==110000.  In
              that case, ppnnmmnnoorrmm changes one of the values by 1 to make it le‐
              gal.   In the example, ppnnmmnnoorrmm would either make the black value
              99 or the white value 101.

              Before Netpbm 10.43 (June 2008), ppnnmmnnoorrmm fails if the  --wwppeerrcceenntt
              and/or --bbppeerrcceenntt values specify an overlap.

              The stretch points are further constrained by the --mmaaxxeexxppaanndd op‐
              tion.  Sometimes, too much contrast is a bad thing.  If your in‐
              tensities  are  all  concentrated in the middle, --bbppeerrcceenntt==22 and
              --wwppeerrcceenntt==11 might mean that an intensity of 60 gets stretched up
              to  100  and  intensity of 20 gets stretched down to zero, for a
              range expansion of 150% (from a range of 40 to a range of  100).
              That much stretching means two adjacent pixels that used to dif‐
              fer in intensity by 4 units now differ by 10, and that might  be
              unsightly.


       --bbssiinnggllee

              To  specify that the single least brightness in the image should
              stretch to black in the output, specify  --bbssiinnggllee.   To  specify
              that  the single greatest brightness in the image should stretch
              to white in the output, specify --wwssiinnggllee.  --bbssiinnggllee and --wwssiinnggllee
              were new in Netpbm 10.69 (December 2014).


       --mmaaxxeexxppaanndd

              So  that  you can put a limit on the amount of expansion without
              having to examine the image first, there is the  --mmaaxxeexxppaanndd  op‐
              tion.   It specifies the maximum expansion you will tolerate, as
              an additional percentage.  In the example above, you  could  say
              --mmaaxxeexxppaanndd==5500  to  say  you  want the range to expand by at most
              50%, regardless of your other options.  ppnnmmnnoorrmm figures out what
              intensity  to  stretch  to  full intensity and what intensity to
              stretch to zero intensity as described above,  and  then  raises
              the  former  and lowers the latter as needed to limit the expan‐
              sion to the amount you specified.

              When ppnnmmnnoorrmm limits the  expansion  because  of  --mmaaxxeexxppaanndd,  it
              tells you about it with a message like this:

                  limiting expansion of 150% to 50%



              In  any  case, ppnnmmnnoorrmm tells you exactly what expansion it's do‐
              ing, like this:


                  remapping 25..75 to 0..100



              Before Netpbm 10.26 (December 2004), it was not valid to specify
              both --bbvvaalluuee and --bbppeerrcceenntt or --wwvvaalluuee and --wwppeerrcceenntt.

              --mmaaxxeexxppaanndd was new in Netpbm 10.32 (February 2006).


       --kkeeeepphhuueess

              This option says to keep each pixel the same hue as it is in the
              input; just adjust its brightness.  You normally want this;  the
              only  reason it is not the default behavior is backward compati‐
              bility with a design mistake.

              By default, ppnnmmnnoorrmm normalizes contrast in each component  inde‐
              pendently  (except  that the meaning of the --wwppeerrcceenntt and --bbppeerr‐‐
              cceenntt options are based on the overall brightnesses of  the  col‐
              ors,  not  each  component  taken separately).  So if you have a
              color which is intensely red but dimly green, ppnnmmnnoorrmm would make
              the  red  more intense and the green less intense, so you end up
              with a different hue than you started with.


       --mmiiddvvaalluuee==_N

       --mmiiddddllee==_N

              When you specify --mmiiddvvaalluuee==_N, ppnnmmnnoorrmm uses a quadratic  function
              to  map  old  brightnesses  to new ones, making sure that an old
              brightness of _N becomes 50% bright in the output.  You can over‐
              ride  that  50% default with --mmiiddddllee.  The value of --mmiiddddllee is a
              floating point number in the range 0 through 1 with 0 being full
              darkness  and  1 full brightness.  If your --mmiiddvvaalluuee and --mmiiddddllee
              indicate an ambiguous or  impossible  quadratic  function  (e.g.
              --mmiiddvvaalluuee  is the same as --bbvvaalluuee, so an infinite number of qua‐
              dratic functions fit), ppnnmmnnoorrmm just ignores your  --mmiiddvvaalluuee  and
              maps linearly.

              --mmiiddvvaalluuee and --mmiiddddllee were new in Netpbm 10.57 (December 2011).

              If  you specify --kkeeeepphhuueess, ppnnmmnnoorrmm would likely leave this pixel
              alone, since its overall brightness is medium.

              --kkeeeepphhuueess can cause clipping, because a certain color may be be‐
              low a target intensity while one of its components is saturated.
              Where that's the case, ppnnmmnnoorrmm uses  the  maximum  representable
              intensity for the saturated component and the pixel ends up with
              less overall intensity, and a different hue, than it is supposed
              to have.

              This option is meaningless on grayscale images.

              When  you _d_o_n_'_t specify --kkeeeepphhuueess, the --lluummiinnoossiittyy, --ccoolloorrvvaalluuee,
              and --ssaattuurraattiioonn options affect the transfer function  (which  is
              the same for all three RGB components), but are meaningless when
              it comes to applying the transfer function (since it is  applied
              to each individual RGB component).

              Before Netpbm 9.25 (March 2002), there was no --kkeeeepphhuueess option.


       --lluummiinnoossiittyy

       --ccoolloorrvvaalluuee

       --ssaattuurraattiioonn

              --lluummiinnoossiittyy,  --ccoolloorrvvaalluuee,  and --ssaattuurraattiioonn determine what prop‐
              erty of the pixels  ppnnmmnnoorrmm  normalizes.   I.e.,  what  kind  of
              brightness.  You cannot specify more than one of these.

              The  --lluummiinnoossiittyy option says to use the luminosity (i.e. the "Y"
              in the YUV or YCbCr color space) as the pixel's brightness.  The
              luminosity is a measure of how bright a human eye would find the
              color, taking into account the fact that the human eye  is  more
              sensitive to some RGB components than others.

              This option is default.

              This option is meaningless on grayscale images.

              Before  Netpbm 10.28 (August 2005), there was no --lluummiinnoossiittyy op‐
              tion, but its meaning was still the default.

              Before Netpbm 10.28 (August 2005), there was no --ccoolloorrvvaalluuee  op‐
              tion.

              The --ccoolloorrvvaalluuee option says to use the color value (i.e. the "V"
              in the HSV color space) as the pixel's  brightness.   The  color
              value  is  the  gamma-adjusted intensity of the most intense RGB
              component.

              This option is meaningless on grayscale images.

              Before Netpbm 10.28 (August 2005), there was no --ccoolloorrvvaalluuee  op‐
              tion.

              The  --ssaattuurraattiioonn option says to use the saturation (i.e. the "S"
              in the HSV color space) as the pixel's brightness.  The  satura‐
              tion  is the ratio of the intensity of the most intense RGB com‐
              ponent to the difference between the intensities of the most and
              least intense RGB component (all intensities gamma-adjusted).

              In  this case, "brightness" is more of a metaphor than anything.
              "bright" means saturated and "dark" means unsaturated.

              This option is meaningless on grayscale images.

              Before Netpbm 10.28 (August 2005), there was no --ccoolloorrvvaalluuee  op‐
              tion.




